{
  "text": " I'm gonna tell you what I use and how I address these patients. Number one, we saw everybody in person. So I got to see them face to face. I'm a face to face doctor, I'm not a telemedicine doctor. I have to see this disease for myself. So those who presented with basically just upper URI symptoms cough, not really cough, but more runny nose, headache, fever, those patients got hydroxychloroquine. We started them on 400 milligrams twice a day on day one, and then 200 milligrams three times a day, day two through five. That's number one drug. Number two, we gave them Zithromax or doxycycline. And number three, we gave them zinc. And the zinc was either the zinc sulfate, the 220, or just regular over the countersink, 25, and we gave that to them twice a day. We also used vitamin D3, 5,000 units a day, vitamin C, 500 milligrams twice a day. And we started using an acetylcysteine, NAC, okay? 600 milligrams a day. That was the foundation of our treatment. That's what it started. If patients had pulmonary symptoms, if they had the chest pain and they had a shortness of breath, we did chest x-rays. We found early on that the chest x-rays were positive for inflammation. So what do we do? We started treating the inflammation. So I gave them six milligrams of decadron, and we started them on inhaled budesonite in the nebulized form. I wanted to stay away from the systemic treatment because I didn't want to really suppress their immune system, but I wanted to treat the inflammation. So that's why we went to the nebulized budesonite form. And amazingly, what happened was we found in two to three days, 99% of our patients were better.",
